<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René Descartes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1596">1596</a>–<a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René Descartes was a French philosopher and mathematician who spent most of his working life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. His Discourse on the Method of 1637 and Meditations of 1641 founded modern rationalist philosophy with the principle 'I think, therefore I am', and he created analytic geometry. He corresponded at length with Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-of-the-palatinate">Elisabeth of the Palatinate</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> in 1650 while serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-queen-of-sweden">Queen Christina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-descartes.jpg" alt="René Descartes" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Frans Hals · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Hals_-_Portret_van_Ren%C3%A9_Descartes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
